1. Create Numeric, Character and Logical Vectors
Aim

To create numeric, character and logical vectors in R and display their type and contents.

Procedure
Create a numeric vector using c().
Create a character vector using c().
Create a logical vector using TRUE and FALSE.
Display the contents and data type using print() and typeof().
Code
# Numeric vector
num <- c(10, 20, 30, 40, 50)

# Character vector
char <- c("Apple", "Banana", "Mango", "Orange")

# Logical vector
logical <- c(TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE)

# Display contents and type
print(num)
print(typeof(num))

print(char)
print(typeof(char))

print(logical)
print(typeof(logical))
Result

Thus, numeric, character and logical vectors were created successfully and their contents and types were displayed.

2. Create Labeled Matrices
Aim

To create labeled matrices of sizes 5×4, 3×3 and 2×2 using row-wise and column-wise filling.

Procedure
Create matrices using the matrix() function.
Use byrow=TRUE for row-wise filling.
Use byrow=FALSE for column-wise filling.
Assign row and column names.
Display the matrices.
Code
# 5 x 4 matrix - filled by row
m1 <- matrix(1:20, nrow=5, ncol=4, byrow=TRUE)
rownames(m1) <- paste0("R", 1:5)
colnames(m1) <- paste0("C", 1:4)

# 3 x 3 matrix - filled by column
m2 <- matrix(1:9, nrow=3, ncol=3, byrow=FALSE)
rownames(m2) <- paste0("R", 1:3)
colnames(m2) <- paste0("C", 1:3)

# 2 x 2 matrix - filled by row
m3 <- matrix(1:4, nrow=2, ncol=2, byrow=TRUE)
rownames(m3) <- c("A", "B")
colnames(m3) <- c("X", "Y")

print(m1)
print(m2)
print(m3)
Result

Thus, labeled 5×4, 3×3 and 2×2 matrices were created and displayed successfully.

