7. Factors, Random Integers and FizzBuzz
Aim

To find the factors of a number, generate random integers and implement the FizzBuzz logic using R.

Procedure
Use a loop to find factors of a number.
Generate 10 random integers between -50 and 50.
Use a loop from 1 to 100.
Apply FizzBuzz conditions using the modulus operator.
Code
# Find factors
n <- 24
factors <- c()

for(i in 1:n) {
  if(n %% i == 0) {
    factors <- c(factors, i)
  }
}

print(factors)

# Generate 10 random integers
random_numbers <- sample(-50:50, 10)
print(random_numbers)

# FizzBuzz
for(i in 1:100) {
  if(i %% 15 == 0) {
    print("FizzBuzz")
  } else if(i %% 3 == 0) {
    print("Fizz")
  } else if(i %% 5 == 0) {
    print("Buzz")
  } else {
    print(i)
  }
}
Result

Thus, the factors were found, random integers were generated, and FizzBuzz logic was implemented successfully.

8. Generate Random Numbers from Normal Distribution
Aim

To generate random numbers from a normal distribution and count their occurrences based on specified ranges.

Procedure
Generate 100 random numbers using rnorm().
Set the mean and standard deviation.
Count values greater than 50 and less than 50.
Group the values into intervals using cut() and count them.
Code
set.seed(123)

# Generate normal random numbers
x <- rnorm(100, mean=50, sd=10)

# Display numbers
print(x)

# Count occurrences
greater_50 <- sum(x > 50)
less_50 <- sum(x < 50)

print(greater_50)
print(less_50)

# Count values in intervals
counts <- table(
  cut(x, breaks=c(-Inf,40,50,60,Inf))
)

print(counts)
Result

Thus, normally distributed random numbers were generated and their occurrences in different ranges were counted successfully.

