16. Explore USArrests Dataset
Aim

To analyze the USArrests dataset using summary statistics, maximum and minimum values, correlation, filtering and graphical visualization.

Procedure
Load the USArrests dataset.
Calculate summary statistics.
Find the state with the largest rape arrest rate.
Find maximum and minimum murder rates.
Calculate correlation between features.
Identify states above median assault arrests and bottom 25% murder arrests.
Generate graphs.
Code
# Load dataset
data <- USArrests

# Summary statistics
print(summary(data))

# Mean
print(colMeans(data))

# Standard deviation
print(sapply(data, sd))

# State with largest rape arrests
state_rape <- rownames(data)[which.max(data$Rape)]

print(state_rape)
print(max(data$Rape))

# Maximum and minimum murder rates
print(max(data$Murder))
print(min(data$Murder))

# Correlation
print(cor(data))

# States above median Assault
median_assault <- median(data$Assault)

above_median <- data[
  data$Assault > median_assault,
]

print(above_median)

# Bottom 25% for Murder
q25 <- quantile(data$Murder, 0.25)

bottom25 <- data[
  data$Murder <= q25,
]

print(bottom25)

# Histogram
hist(
  data$Murder,
  main="Murder Arrests",
  xlab="Murder"
)

# Density plot
plot(
  density(data$Murder),
  main="Density of Murder Arrests"
)

# Scatter plot
plot(
  data$Murder,
  data$Assault,
  main="Murder vs Assault",
  xlab="Murder",
  ylab="Assault"
)

# Bar graph
barplot(
  data$Murder[1:10],
  names.arg=rownames(data)[1:10],
  las=2,
  main="Murder Arrests"
)
Result

Thus, the USArrests dataset was successfully analyzed using statistical calculations, filtering, correlation and different visualization techniques.

