21. Iris Train/Test Split and Logistic Regression
Aim

To randomly divide the Iris dataset into 80% training and 20% testing data, build a logistic regression model, predict the class and evaluate it using a confusion matrix.

Procedure
Load the Iris dataset.
Convert the three-class Species variable into a binary classification problem: Setosa and Non-Setosa.
Randomly split the dataset into 80% training and 20% testing data.
Build a logistic regression model using Petal Length and Petal Width.
Predict the test data.
Create a confusion matrix and calculate accuracy.
Code
# Load Iris dataset
data <- iris

# Convert to binary classification
data$Species <- ifelse(
  data$Species == "setosa",
  "setosa",
  "non-setosa"
)

data$Species <- factor(data$Species)

# Random 80/20 split
set.seed(123)

n <- nrow(data)

train_index <- sample(
  1:n,
  size=0.8*n
)

train <- data[train_index,]
test <- data[-train_index,]

# Logistic regression
model <- glm(
  Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width,
  data=train,
  family=binomial
)

# Model summary
print(summary(model))

# Predict probabilities
prob <- predict(
  model,
  newdata=test,
  type="response"
)

# Convert probability into class
pred <- ifelse(
  prob > 0.5,
  "setosa",
  "non-setosa"
)

pred <- factor(
  pred,
  levels=levels(test$Species)
)

# Confusion matrix
confusion_matrix <- table(
  Actual=test$Species,
  Predicted=pred
)

print(confusion_matrix)

# Calculate accuracy
accuracy <- sum(diag(confusion_matrix)) /
            sum(confusion_matrix)

print(accuracy)
Result

Thus, the Iris dataset was successfully divided into training and testing sets, a logistic regression model was built, predictions were made and model performance was evaluated using a confusion matrix and accuracy.
